  "verbalization": "Cefprozil, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is implicated in the treatment of streptococcal infections through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Cefprozil may be repurposed for use in streptococcal infections by targeting bacterial pathogens through its antibacterial activity. The visible paths indicate that Cefprozil is associated with indications such as tonsillitis and infectious otitis media, both of which are linked to streptococcal infections. These indications are further connected to antibiotics like dirithromycin and loracarbef, which are known to inhibit streptococcal pathogens. The presence of these indications, along with the final scores indicating a plausible mechanistic link, suggests that Cefprozil may exert its antibacterial effect by disrupting bacterial cell walls or inhibiting essential metabolic processes in streptococcal organisms. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Cefprozil could be a viable candidate for repurposing in the treatment of streptococcal infections, provided further experimental validation is conducted to confirm its efficacy and safety in this context.",
